<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 624–<a href="/years/641">641</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kubja Vishnuvardhana was the founder of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> in coastal Andhra. A younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> of the Chalukyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, he was made viceroy of the Vengi region after its conquest from the Vishnukundina dynasty around 624. He soon ruled independently, and his line governed Vengi for about four centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vishnuvardhana, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> as Bittarasa, was the younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> of the Vatapi Chalukyas. Pulakeshin conquered the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, the coastal districts of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, defeating the remnants of the Vishnukundina kingdom, and appointed his brother viceroy there. The scholar Das Kornel holds that Vishnuvardhana himself subdued the Vishnukundina king, possibly Janasraya Madhava Varman, and carved out a kingdom of his own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His kingdom stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a> to Visakhapatnam, and he took the title Vishamasiddhi, conqueror of difficulties. Scholars do not agree on how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> became a separate kingdom. It has often been assumed that Pulakeshin formally granted his brother independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, yet the Kopparam plates state that Vishnuvardhana ruled only as his brother's subordinate, and the records show good relations between the brothers, which rules out a revolt.</p><p>Another explanation looks to the disasters of Pulakeshin's last years. The Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> inflicted a crushing defeat on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, burnt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> and killed Pulakeshin, whose five sons then fought one another until the third, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-i">Vikramaditya I</a>, became king around 642 and restored order. In this view the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> viceroyalty became an independent kingdom on Pulakeshin's death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Vishnuvardhana took part in his brother's wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i">Narasimhavarman I</a> and probably lost his life in battle in 641. His son Jayasimha I succeeded him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> outlived the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Vatapi</a> line by many generations and kept a very close relationship with the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kubja Vishnuvardhana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Princes of the royal house governed distant conquests as viceroys: Pulakeshin II installed his brother <b>Kubja Vishnuvardhana</b> in Vengi, where he founded the line of the Eastern Chalukyas around 624, and his son Dharashraya Jayasimha governed southern Gujarat, founding another branch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 624 Pulakeshin II of the Chalukyas of Badami, having conquered the Vishnukundina dynasty, made his younger brother <b>Kubja Vishnuvardhana</b> viceroy of the Vengi region between the Krishna and Godavari rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 7th century Pulakeshin II of the Chalukyas of Badami conquered the Vengi region and gave it to his brother <b>Kubja Vishnuvardhana</b>, who founded the Eastern Chalukyas around 624.</blockquote><a href="/state/vishnukundina-dynasty">Vishnukundina dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
